the world of inside haunts you from  start to finish it's a world broken at  the hands of technological progress work  out and enslaved people shuffle  mindlessly through the streets overseers  dressed in masks and black clothes stand  guard waiting for one of the slaves to  fall out of line inside is the second  game from limbo developer play dead and  like limbo the gameplay is simple you  walk jump and grab objects in order to  solve puzzles and overcome obstacles  however inside is primarily about your  journey through a tyrannical  impenetrable and apocalyptic world over  the course of a few hours you descend  ever deeper into the heart of a  malicious and immense construct as you  try and discover what is hidden inside  and as you journey towards that secret  you experience stretches of quiet and  calm punctuated by flashes of absurd  violence or cruelty the game encourages  you to relish these often shocking or  brutal twists and they make you want to  know more and these moments remain vivid  in my memory even a few days after  completing the game a mindless sort of  figures followed me off a cliff only to  slam into the ground later a wispy  feminine creature tenaciously stalked me  through underwater regions I also led my  character to countless deaths that were  immediate and gruesome I loved the game  most during its quiet lulls when the  oppressive feeling of the world was most  apparent but I sometimes couldn't help  but laugh at the sheer ridiculousness of  these in-your-face moments as you move  through its world you're confronted with  puzzles that you must solve to progress  well most of these are fun that comes a  time when inside leans too heavily on  its puzzles to keep you engaged in these  moments I felt that my driving  motivation has shifted from exploring  the world to simply flipping the right  switch fortunately this issue evaporates  as the game enters its final act when  the world and puzzles again form a  cohesive bond inside is at its best when  it doesn't feel like you're doing  puzzles at all but rather avoiding  obstacles and finding paths through a  dangerous space and as inside nears its  conclusion the puzzles reinforce the  sense of exploration that defines the  rest of the game this feeling is  enhanced even more by insides visual and  sound design the game is gorgeous with  assemble but evocative art style defined  by muted colors and featureless figures  insides environments are richly detailed  and full of motion and secrets and this  style is complemented by insides often  breathtaking use of sound of music you  never hear voices but each setting has  its own unique noises music is used  sparingly but when it swells even minor  events like exiting a building became  points fixed in my memory  all of it the setting the sound and the  beautiful art builds to the discovery of  the secret of this compound but what I  found at the end almost ruined the  entire experience Sala T was thrown at  the window and I was left reeling unable  to process the turn but as time passed  those final moments grew on me I still  find the ending strange but upon playing  through inside a second time I found  endearing elements that fit with the  overall story more effectively than I  first thought  it's both humorous and also cathartic  releasing in one scene all of the  tension that have built up over the rest  of the game this is a beautiful in  haunting game and it's a worthy  follow-up to Limbo detailed environments  convey the bizarre world that you travel  through introspective moments are filled  with minimalistic sound design and  calculated touches of music and the  things that you must do to complete your  journey force you to confront the  realities of humanity freedom and  existence is enough motivation to return  to insides dystopia  you
 

